  "query_preview": "..\nSYNOPTIC SETUP:\n\nLarge scale flow aloft from the southwest generally over our\nregion will continue to direct a few weak storm systems across\neastern Montana, but some warm high pressure influence will be\nfelt between these systems. That will be enough to allow daytime\ntemperatures to climb into the mid to upper 80s again by Thursday. \n\nIn the meantime, the main disturbance expected to impact our\nregion will be overnight tonight through Wednesday. The \ntrajectory of this system will take the best chances for \naccumulating precipitation near, along and south of the Big Sheep \nMountains of eastern Montana. Some isolated locations may see as \nmuch as 0.25 inch of rainfall.\n\nSometime on Saturday this weekend, a pronounced low pressure\nsystem from British Columbia will begin to push through the\nnorthern Rockies of Idaho and western Montana. Models are\ncurrently showing not much rainfall will that system, so most of\nthe noticeable change early next week will be gusty winds\nassociated with the cold front on Sat/Sun and generally lower \ntemperatures moving on from there.\n\nBLM\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nConfidence is generally average to above average for increasing\nclouds and rain chances after midnight tonight. Lower confidence\nfor the finer details of exact placement and amount of rainfall,\nas most of the hi-res model data shows a scattered showery nature\nto the precipitation. \n\nDid not see any glaring reasons to deviate from the NBM. \n\nBLM",
